{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful noble who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He became the count at a very young age after his father died in battle. His early life involved a long regency period because he was too young to rule alone. The kingdom of Jerusalem and the county of Tripoli faced many threats from Muslim forces during this time. Raymond served as a regent for King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. This role placed him at the center of political struggles within the Crusader states. He often clashed with other nobles like Guy of Lusignan over control of the kingdom. The tension between these factions would eventually lead to disaster at the Battle of Hattin.\n\n*Early Life and Regency*\nRaymond was born around 1140 to Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was assassinated by the Assassins when Raymond was just ten years old. A regency council governed the county until he reached adulthood at the age of fifteen. He married Eschiva of Bures to secure his position and expand his influence. This marriage brought him control over important territories in Galilee. He later became involved in the succession crisis of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His support for the royal line against Guy of Lusignan marked a pivotal moment in his career.\n\n*Conflict with Guy of Lusignan*\nTensions rose sharply when Guy of Lusignan became king through marriage to Sibylla. Raymond opposed Guy because he believed the king was incompetent and reckless. The two men led opposing factions that paralyzed the government of Jerusalem. Raymond even allied with Saladin temporarily to counter the power of Guy and his supporters. This act of betrayal caused many to view him as a traitor despite his political motivations. The split within the Christian leadership weakened their defenses against the rising Muslim power.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Later Years*\nThe feud between the factions culminated in the catastrophic Battle of Hattin in 1187. Guy led the army into a trap set by Saladin while Raymond tried to prevent the disaster. Raymond managed to escape the slaughter but watched his allies and the kingdom fall apart. He returned to Tripoli to defend his own county from the invading forces. Saladin besieged the city but failed to capture it due to strong defenses. Raymond died shortly after the siege in 1187 or 1189 depending on historical accounts. His death marked the end of an era for the Latin East. The county eventually passed to his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond remains a controversial figure in Crusader history due to his shifting alliances. His story illustrates the internal divisions that plagued the Crusader states before their final collapse.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0018055636}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.003955428}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.1546459}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.005319833}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.6294913}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.006613019}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -1.396043}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.07772945}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.028339194}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00049197854}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0074159624}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.09362851}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.018846508}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -2.02656e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -9.536789e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.050980024}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.34343985}, {"token": " became", "logprob": -1.6446165}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.32720143}, {"token": " count", "logprob": -0.26702058}, {"token": " at", "logprob": -0.29388875}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00031464282}, {"token":